    draft: "Concise summary from three web sources: a 12-month randomized trial in the New England Journal of Medicine, a randomized early time-restricted eating trial (JAMA Internal Medicine, full text via LSU), and NIDDK clinician guidance.\n**Comparable to calorie restriction, with the benefit driven by eating less.** In a 12-month randomized trial of 139 adults with obesity, eight-hour time-restricted eating (8:00 a.m.–4:00 p.m.) plus calorie restriction produced mean weight loss of 8.00 kg versus 6.14 kg with daily calorie restriction alone, a between-group difference that was not statistically significant (source:drj7c5:version:f354ad6bebd9ae168101f3c207e2d8db0f2113d2cb43c2aa0903527b32ba9c36:passage:3818-4518).\n**Timing may add modest benefit.** The NIDDK expert view is that \"I don't think it's the fasting.\nI think they're just eating less, so they're losing weight,\" with the main benefit being improved blood glucose regulation — reductions in fasting insulin and improved insulin resistance in people with obesity and prediabetes, and sometimes lower blood pressure (source:1axapze:version:f2a23933710108490535eef858d2bd2c90e560f51382409709235a49c42b4066:passage:4521-5220).\nBy contrast, the JAMA Internal Medicine trial reported that a *very early* eight-hour eating window combined with energy restriction produced more weight and fat loss and improvements in fasting insulin, insulin resistance, blood pressure, and oxidative-stress markers versus a later control window (source:v38ykx:version:37b9cbcab96943bb3c0a74024f842e44d017fa4d5e164c25bdf67f3183a0a36a:passage:657-1357).\nThe same trial's authors state it is \"unclear how effective intermittent fasting is for losing weight and body fat, and the effects may depend on the timing of\" the eating window (source:v38ykx:version:37b9cbcab96943bb3c0a74024f842e44d017fa4d5e164c25bdf67f3183a0a36a:passage:657-1357).\n\"Early research suggests that intermittent fasting may help some people lose weight.\nHowever, scientists are still studying how intermittent fasting affects people with diabetes.\" (source:1axapze:version:f2a23933710108490535eef858d2bd2c90e560f51382409709235a49c42b4066:passage:96-796)\nOn glycemic outcomes: \"Three or four different studies all show similar weight loss and similar improvements in A1C.\nI think it does work.\" (source:1axapze:version:f2a23933710108490535eef858d2bd2c90e560f51382409709235a49c42b4066:passage:8708-9407)\nDurability: \"We found that participants could maintain their weight loss when they stuck to an 8- to 10-hour eating window\" over a 12-month program (source:1axapze:version:f2a23933710108490535eef858d2bd2c90e560f51382409709235a49c42b4066:passage:7175-7875).\nSafety: patients with type 2 diabetes \"must work closely with their doctors to adjust medications, specifically sulfonylureas and insulin\" because fasting windows change the eating pattern (source:1axapze:version:f2a23933710108490535eef858d2bd2c90e560f51382409709235a49c42b4066:passage:9236-9935), and clinicians worry about older adults \"because of the risk of excessive muscle mass loss\" (source:1axapze:version:f2a23933710108490535eef858d2bd2c90e560f51382409709235a49c42b4066:passage:6873-7573).\nThe three sampled sources cover weight loss, A1C, fasting insulin, blood pressure/oxidative stress, and short-term safety in adults with obesity or type 2 diabetes, but they conflict on whether meal timing adds benefit beyond calorie restriction: the NEJM trial found no significant difference versus calorie restriction alone while the early-window trial and the NIDDK commentary suggest timing-dependent advantages, and this disagreement remains unresolved.\nThe evidence cannot answer questions about effects beyond 12 months — the NIDDK expert notes \"Overall, the data are promising on intermittent fasting in type 2 diabetes.\nWe just need much longer studies with larger sample sizes.\" (source:1axapze:version:f2a23933710108490535eef858d2bd2c90e560f51382409709235a49c42b4066:passage:3500-4200) — nor about older adults (muscle-loss risk needs study), people with a history of eating disorders, pregnant women, type 1 diabetes, cardiovascular events, or cancer outcomes.\n\"Calorie Restriction with or without Time-Restricted Eating in Weight Loss.\" *New England Journal of Medicine*, April 21, 2022; 386:1495-1504. https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2114833 (passages: source:drj7c5:version:f354ad6bebd9ae168101f3c207e2d8db0f2113d2cb43c2aa0903527b32ba9c36:passage:2900-3139; source:drj7c5:version:f354ad6bebd9ae168101f3c207e2d8db0f2113d2cb43c2aa0903527b32ba9c36:passage:3818-4518)\nJamshed H, Steger FL, Bryan DR, et al.\n\"Effectiveness of Early Time-Restricted Eating for Weight Loss, Fat Loss, and Cardiometabolic Health in Adults With Obesity.\" *JAMA Internal Medicine*, 2022. https://repository.lsu.edu/clinical_research_pubs/227 (passage: source:v38ykx:version:37b9cbcab96943bb3c0a74024f842e44d017fa4d5e164c25bdf67f3183a0a36a:passage:657-1357)\n\"What Can You Tell Your Patients About Intermittent Fasting and Type 2 Diabetes?\" May 1, 2024. https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/professionals/diabetes-discoveries-practice/patients-intermittent-fasting (passages: source:1axapze:version:f2a23933710108490535eef858d2bd2c90e560f51382409709235a49c42b4066:passage:96-796; source:1axapze:version:f2a23933710108490535eef858d2bd2c90e560f51382409709235a49c42b4066:passage:3500-4200; source:1axapze:version:f2a23933710108490535eef858d2bd2c90e560f51382409709235a49c42b4066:passage:4521-5220; source:1axapze:version:f2a23933710108490535eef858d2bd2c90e560f51382409709235a49c42b4066:passage:6873-7573; source:1axapze:version:f2a23933710108490535eef858d2bd2c90e560f51382409709235a49c42b4066:passage:7175-7875; source:1axapze:version:f2a23933710108490535",
